WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:07.500
Hi, my name is Chris Gagne, and I'm an Agile coach. Today, I'm going to talk about The Tradeoff Matrix

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.090
When working on a software project there are four key levers that you can manipulate:

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.320
Scope, Schedule, Resources, and Quality

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.500
Scope is the total quantity of functionality and features you plan to develop.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.180
Schedule is when you make your releases available to your end-users.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.600
Resources is the number of people and teams working on your project.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.760
Quality is quality, including the number of bugs,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.420
documentation, amount of technical debt, and the degree of automation in your code

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.360
Let's take quality off the table as a lever right from the start.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.160
In the long run, quality is free.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.360
This is because if you're compromising quality in the short term to ship a given scope on a given date

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:02.700
with a fixed number of resources, you will incur a lot of technical debt that's going to make development slower in the long run.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.820
So never compromising quality. You don't need to do overdo it either, but find the right balance.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.620
That leaves Scope, Schedule, and Resources

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.320
We don't have much information at the beginning of the project

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.240
so any predictions we try to make about the delivery of a project will be wrong.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.830
In the beginning of a project, these estimates can easily be off by a factor of four or more.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.580
So if we try to fix Scope, Schedule, and Resources,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.540
we will almost certainly have to compromise on work-life balance and quality.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.040
And again, neither of these approaches work 
in the long run.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:45.800
So the best approach is to negotiate with stakeholders in advance as to what will be Fixed, Firm, and Flexible

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.960
with respect to Scope, Schedule, and Resources.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.100
Only one of these can be Fixed, Firm and Flexible.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.720
Therefore, there are six possible approaches. My favorite approach is Fixed Resources,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:05.040
Firm Date, and Flexible Scope. I like having fixed resources because according to Brooks' Law

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.540
Adding people to a late project makes it later. This is because it takes time to ramp people up.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:19.860
Additional people on the team produce a combinatorial explosion of communication overhead and the work is not necessarily divisible further.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.540
Having a Firm Date, keeps stakeholders happy and gives us a healthy sense of urgency.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.540
We can always choose to move the date later if we wish.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.740
Finally, having a Flexible Scope demands that we think very carefully about

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.720
incremental value delivery and user-centric iterative software development.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.500
So take the time to talk to your team and stakeholders about the Tradeoff Matrix today.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:52.580
Then you will know exactly what to do when push comes to shove and you need to make tough decisions together.

